arm64: dts: h713: hy310 - PB5 to the board manager, not a hog

PB5 enables the LED boost and the fan rail together. As a gpio-hog it
stayed high in every standby: the fan ran (PB5 high gives ~4800 rpm, low
0 rpm, measured 11.09.2026) and, very probably, the lamp stayed lit; the
board manager's switch for it was dead code, because its fan0 node named
no fan-power-gpio, and its "fan power off" line claimed a switch that
never happened (crust-20261007 review KD-7, N-05; W1 and D1 of the crust
release design).

On the HY310 the hog is disabled - gpiolib only hogs available children
- and fan0 names PB5 as fan-power-gpio, where the board manager reads it
(of_get_named_gpio on the fan node). The board manager requests it high
at probe; until then PB5 keeps the high level U-Boot set. Switching it
in suspend_late and back in resume_early is the driver's part (D1).

The HY300 Pro and the HY200 QZ713DF A1 include the HY310's file and get
the hog back, with fan0's fan-power-gpio deleted: on the HY300 Pro PB5
must never go low (analysis A14), and neither board has a measurement of
it switched.

The two dtsi comments that said PB5 must never be driven low (the hog's
and the backlight's) now say who holds it on which board, so that nobody
"repairs" the standby switch back into a hog.

Wall darkness, tacho and supply current in a standby are stage L of the
design, not measured. With a board manager that already switches an
owned rail (0188) but does not yet hold off the stall check after resume
(D1), this tree is not for the line.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
